git fetch origin
git shortlog -sne <my-commit>..origin/master

This prints one line per author with their commit count, so you'll see right away who has the most of the 200+.

2. Same count, without merge commits (merges can inflate the numbers)

bash
git shortlog -sne --no-merges <my-commit>..origin/master

3. Full list: hash, date, author, message

bash
git log --no-merges --pretty=format:'%h  %ad  %an  %s' --date=short <my-commit>..origin/master

To save it to a file and show Rajesh:

bash
git log --no-merges --pretty=format:'%h,%ad,%an,%s' --date=short <my-commit>..origin/master > commits_since_mine.csv

4. Only commits that touched the same file as yours (usually the useful view)

bash
git log --no-merges --pretty=format:'%h  %ad  %an  %s' --date=short <my-commit>..origin/master -- <path/to/file>

git rev-parse 4edda1e7b^{tree} HEAD^{tree}

If you don't have your commit hash handy:

bash
git log --oneline --author="AM71511" -10 origin/master


git log --oneline -n 3 HEAD
git diff --stat 4edda1e7b HEAD | tail -5
git diff --summary 4edda1e7b HEAD | head -20
